A [Next.js](https://nextjs.org) (App Router) application written in TypeScript and styled with Tailwind CSS.

## Prerequisites

- Node.js 26.x (see `.nvmrc` and the `engines` field in `package.json`)
- npm (bundled with Node.js)

If you use [nvm](https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm) or [fnm](https://github.com/Schniz/fnm), run `nvm install && nvm use` (or `fnm use`) in the repository root.

## Getting started

```bash
npm ci        # install exact dependency versions from package-lock.json
npm run dev   # start the dev server at http://localhost:3000
```

## Commands

| Command                | What it does                                                     |
| ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `npm run dev`          | Start the development server                                     |
| `npm run build`        | Create a production build                                        |
| `npm run start`        | Serve the production build                                       |
| `npm run lint`         | Run ESLint                                                       |
| `npm run typecheck`    | Generate Next.js route types, then run `tsc --noEmit`            |
| `npm run test`         | Run unit tests once with Vitest (non-interactive)                |
| `npm run format`       | Format the codebase with Prettier                                |
| `npm run format:check` | Verify formatting without changing files                         |
| `npm run check`        | Run lint, typecheck, format check, tests, and a production build |

## Validation gate

`npm run check` is the standard validation gate. It stops at the first failing step and exits nonzero, so it is suitable for CI. Run it and get a zero exit code before considering any change complete. The GitHub Actions workflow in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs `npm ci` and `npm run check` on every pull request and on pushes to `main`.

## AI-assisted development

`AGENTS.md` contains the project-specific instructions for AI coding agents (working agreement, secure-coding rules, and the commands above). `CLAUDE.md` imports it. Keep it up to date when commands or conventions change.

## UI components (shadcn/ui)

UI components come from [shadcn/ui](https://ui.shadcn.com) (Radix primitives, neutral theme, CSS variables, Lucide icons). Settings live in `components.json`; generated components live in `src/components/ui/` and are ours to edit.

To add a component, for example `card`:

```bash
npx shadcn@4.23.0 add card   # use a release at least two weeks old
```

Then:

1. Review the diff. The CLI can add files and dependencies and can edit `src/app/globals.css`.
2. Pin any new dependency to an exact version in `package.json`.
3. Run `npx prettier --write` on the new files, then `npm run check`.
4. Import through the alias, for example `import { Card } from "@/components/ui/card"`.

Add only the components the app uses. Theme colors are the CSS variables in `src/app/globals.css`.

{% if zod %}## Validation (Zod)

Shared [Zod](https://zod.dev) schemas live in `src/lib/schemas/` and are exported from
`src/lib/schemas/index.ts`. `appInfoSchema` describes `src/lib/app-info.json` and is tested in
`src/lib/schemas/app-info.test.ts`. Add one schema file and one test per input shape, export it
from the index, and parse untrusted input with `safeParse` on the server; client-side checks are
for UX only.

{% if forms %}## Forms (React Hook Form)

`/forms-example` shows the pattern: [React Hook Form](https://react-hook-form.com) with
`zodResolver` and a shared schema (`exampleFormSchema` in `src/lib/schemas/example-form.ts`), so the
rules are written once and the form's types come from the schema. The form is in
`src/components/forms/example-form.tsx` and is tested with a DOM environment next to it. It does not
save or send anything: pass `onValid` to handle the values, validate again with the same schema on
the server, then delete the example page.

{% endif %}{% if rest_api %}## REST API (v1)

Versioned route handlers live in `src/app/api/v1/`, their request and response schemas in
`src/lib/schemas/v1/` (importable by clients) and the server-only helpers in `src/server/api/`.
`GET /api/v1/app-info` returns the application's name and description, and `POST
/api/v1/examples/validate` is the pattern for an endpoint that accepts input. Errors are RFC 9457
problem details (`application/problem+json`) with stable `code` values. Paths, schemas, status codes,
the error format and the versioning rules are in `docs/api-v1.md`. Handlers are tested by calling
them with `Request` objects, so `npm run check` needs no running server.

{% endif %}{% if has_database %}## Database

A PostgreSQL database for local development, with version-controlled SQL migrations and a typed,
server-only data-access layer. Provider: **{{ database }}**. Start with `docs/database.md`:
`npm run db:init`, `npm run db:up`, `npm run db:migrate`. The sample `app.items` table and its repository
(`src/server/items/`) show the pattern; `npm run test:db` runs the real-database tests. There is no
authentication yet, so nothing in the application's routes reaches the database.

